@scene(40)

// A post rising out of a ball, its junction rounded with an exact fillet.
//
// unionRoundExact(field1, field2, radius, iterations) rounds the seam of a
// union with a rolling-ball fillet of that radius: the surface a ball of the
// radius sweeps as it rolls around the junction touching both solids, here a
// torus tangent to the ball and to the post. It finds the seam's point nearest
// p by Newton steps; three put this fillet within a millionth of a millimetre
// of the torus. The five-argument form, which takes the faces' normals and one
// step, is exact only where the faces are flat: here, where a sphere meets a
// cylinder, its fillet is off by a few percent of the radius. Each step
// evaluates both fields again, so the fillet costs several times the one-step
// form to mesh. Pass the step count as a constant.
//
// Try the slider: radius sets the fillet.

ballRadius = 10;
postRadius = 4;
postHeight = 15;

radius = 2;
radius.min = 0.5;
radius.max = 3;

ball = length(p) - ballRadius;
post = extrude(circleXY([0, 0], postRadius), 0, postHeight, intersectRound(0));

return unionRoundExact(ball, post, radius, 3);
